# ─── Your website ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copy this file to .env.local, fill in the two required values, and you are
# running. Nothing here talks to anybody but your own database.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#   npm install
#   npm run db:push
#   npm run dev
#
# Then open http://localhost:3000 and the setup wizard will ask you what kind
# of business this is.

# ─── Database (required) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A Postgres database. Free ones that work out of the box: neon.tech,
# supabase.com. Paste the connection string they give you.
#
# On a serverless host (Vercel, Netlify), use the POOLED connection string —
# the direct one runs out of connections as soon as you have real visitors.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname

# How many database connections one server instance may hold. Leave it alone
# unless your database provider complains about too many connections.
# SITE_DB_POOL_MAX=5

# ─── Sessions (required before you go live) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# This signs the login cookies. It must be at least 32 random characters, and
# it must be secret. Generate one:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# In development you can leave it empty and a fixed development key is used.
# In production the site refuses to start without it, on purpose.
#
# Changing this signs everybody out. That is your emergency lever if you ever
# think somebody has got hold of a session.
SITE_JWT_SECRET=

# ─── The override code ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One code that opens the order desk no matter what the passwords are, and the
# only thing that opens the diagnostics screen. It is there for the day you or
# your staff are locked out.
#
# A default is compiled into the source. CHANGE IT HERE before you go live —
# the diagnostics screen will keep warning you until you do. Every time it is
# used, a row is written to the audit log with the time and the address.
SITE_MASTER_CODE=

# ─── The order desk address ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Your site has two halves:
#
#   yourbusiness.com          the shop window, for customers
#   orders.yourbusiness.com   the order desk, for you and your staff
#
# Point both at this deployment in your DNS, and the second one lands on the
# desk automatically. Change the label below if you would rather it were
# admin. or desk.
#
# Before DNS is set up — and on any preview URL — the desk is also reachable at
# /console on the main address. It is behind a login either way.
# SITE_ORDERS_SUBDOMAIN=orders
